.TH GIT-PR-LAND 1 "2026-10-08" "git-tools" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
git-pr-land \- land one ready GitHub pull request
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git pr-land
.RI [ options ]
.I number|url|branch
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git pr-land
verifies that one GitHub pull request is ready, merges it, syncs the base
branch locally, and deletes the local pull request head when it is exactly the
landed head, or an earlier snapshot of it that tracks the pull request head. A
clean linked worktree holding that branch is removed first, under the same
gates as
.BR git-cleanup-repo (1);
the current worktree, a worktree with local content, an active operation, or a
process using it, and a head with commits the landed head lacks all keep the
branch. Deletion is also refused while any worktree holds an index or HEAD
lock, as a checkout in progress does. Remote heads are never deleted; they are
reported for manual cleanup, with their exact object ID when a configured
remote maps to the head repository.
.PP
URL targets are required to belong to the current GitHub repository before the
pull request is looked up or merged.
.PP
Numeric and branch targets, checks, merge, and post-merge verification are
pinned to the checkout repository and host regardless of ambient GH_REPO or
GH_HOST values.
.PP
The requested pull request number, returned structured number, and canonical URL
must agree before mutation. Draft and cross-repository fields, head repository,
and head/base refs must also be internally consistent. Post-merge verification
is bound to the same pull request number and topology before cleanup can
continue. Stack-driven landing revalidates its expected topology immediately
before the merge. The merge is pinned to the structured head OID with
.BR \-\-match\-head\-commit ,
and an exactly mapped live remote head must equal that OID. The base fetch URL
must map to the checkout repository before the irreversible merge. Preflight
and post-merge synchronization fetch only the exact refs/heads/base source and
merge its captured OID, without trusting named-remote refspecs, tracking refs,
colliding tags, or importing unrelated tags.
.PP
The local base is fast-forwarded in the worktree that has it checked out. Only
the main worktree may switch to the base; a linked worktree never does, and
advances only the base ref, by fast-forward, when nothing has it checked out.
Updates to a checkout other than the current one are always reported. Neither
the switch nor the fast-forward overwrites an ignored file with a newly tracked
one; the base is then left for the user, and the diagnostic says the pull
request already merged. The
command refuses before merging when the worktree that would receive the base
has local changes or an active operation, or when a rebase or bisect reserves
the base. A base checked out in a main worktree whose files live outside its
Git directory (such as a
.B core.worktree
checkout) is never touched or inspected and is reported instead.
.PP
The command refuses to run, before merging, in a main checkout that Git
reaches only through
.BR GIT_DIR / GIT_WORK_TREE
or
.BR core.worktree ,
such as a dotfiles checkout of the home directory; land from a linked worktree
of that repository instead. Separate Git directories and submodules, whose
.B .git
file leads back to their Git directory, are not affected.
.PP
Git has no portable primitive that serializes branch deletion with a checkout
that already resolved the ref but has not updated its worktree HEAD, so local
deletion is refused while any worktree holds an index or HEAD lock, which a
checkout in progress does. Remote deletion is manual: a repository-scoped PR
inventory cannot prove that a fork head is unused by PRs targeting another base
repository, and a new consumer can appear after any query.
.PP
This command intentionally does not discover or land stacks. Use
.BR git-pr-land-stack (1)
for stack orchestration.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-method " " \fImethod\fR
Merge method:
.BR squash ,
.BR merge ,
or
.BR rebase .
Defaults to
.BR squash .
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-branch
Keep the local pull request head and its worktree.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print actions without changing anything. A dry run makes the same local checks
as a real run and, where the real run would refuse before merging, reports
.B would refuse:
and the reason, and exits 1.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Pull request was landed.
.TP
.B 1
The pull request was not landed: it is not ready, the merge failed, a
precondition refused it (local changes or an active operation where the base
would be updated, a local base that cannot fast-forward, a checkout reached
only through GIT_DIR, GIT_WORK_TREE, or core.worktree, or gh not
authenticated), or a lookup
(the repository, the pull request, its checks, or a remote) failed. Also when
the pull request merged but post-merge cleanup is incomplete; the diagnostic
then explicitly states that the pull request is already merged.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, not inside a Git repository, a bare repository, or
.B gh
not installed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git-pr-ready (1),
.BR git-pr-stack (1),
.BR git-pr-land-stack (1)
